  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 227 AM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nFor Wednesday, slightly warmer temperatures are expected north \nof the frontal boundary that will be situated south across \nnorthern KS. WAA, greatest across western Nebraska will lead to \na return of the lower 80s for areas generally west of HWY 83. \nAreas along and east will still remain cooler with highs in the \n70s. A SW trof will begin to advance eastward from the the \nwestern CONUS will increase lift in the evening and overnight \nhours, increasing the chance for showers and thunderstorms. At \nthis time the environment does not look favorable for severe \nweather across the forecast area with marginal CAPE values \naround 500 to 800 J/kg and weak shear. The better environment \nwill be southward into northern KS where CAPE will be at 1000 \nJ/kg or greater and better forcing exist. \n\nAs for precipitation chances the remainder of the forecast period, \nthere will be several weak disturbances that will move through the \nregion with nearly daily precipitation chances possible. At \nthis time the chance for severe weather remains relatively low \nwith weak forcing. Temperatures through the week will generally \nremain near normal for this time of year with highs in the 70s \nto low 80s.",
